* START-HISTORY:
* 14 Sep 26 dm  adopted from SD Core for Windows (W.4 SCRAM phase 2, the
*               owner's conformity rule).  The derivation, the record and the
*               read-back are the port's.  Its elevated-helper fallback is not:
*               it exists because a Windows token cannot be raised mid-session,
*               and here $cred is root-only and the caller runs with euid 0
*               (CPROC privileged_commands, $MODIFY.PASSWORD).  Record ids are
*               lower case (plan M).
* 30 Aug 26 Windows port - the write falls back to the ELEVATED HELPER; the
*           record is READ BACK and both keys compared.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 68
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - writes a version 2 SCRAM record.
* 13 Aug 26 Windows port - written for the account credential model
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* Derives and stores SCRAM material for an account.  What is written:
*
*   SaltedPassword = PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256(password, salt, iterations, 32)
*   StoredKey      = SHA256(HMAC(SaltedPassword, "Client Key"))
*   ServerKey      = HMAC(SaltedPassword, "Server Key")
*
* THE PASSWORD IS NOT STORED, and neither is ClientKey.  StoredKey is the
* SHA-256 of ClientKey, so holding it does not let the holder authenticate as
* the client - forming a proof needs ClientKey itself, which is the preimage.
* It does let the holder impersonate the SERVER, which is why $cred is
* root:root 0700 (installsdai.sh) and read by nobody else.
*
* This is the only place in the server that runs PBKDF2.  Login does not: it
* holds StoredKey directly and needs two HMACs and a hash, so the cost of a
* high iteration count falls on setting a password, not on connecting.
*
* ***THE CALLER MUST ALREADY HOLD euid 0.***  A session without it cannot open
* $cred, and this answers ER$NVR rather than pretending - the port's "ordinary
* console NO, deliberately" (its secure-cred.ps1), for the same reason: a user
* able to write their own record could write anybody's.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine CRED_SET(account, password, ok)
$catalog !CRED_SET

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h
$include err.h

   ok = @false
   set.status 0

   account = downcase(trim(account))
   if account = '' or password = '' then
      set.status ER$ARGS
      return
   end

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to cred.f else
      set.status ER$NVR
      return
   end

   salt = ''
   call !SD_GET_SALT(salt)
   if salt = '' then
      close cred.f
      set.status ER$ARGS
      return
   end

   iter = SCRAM$ITERATIONS

   salted = SDEXT(password : @FM : salt : @FM : iter : @FM : SCRAM$KEY.LEN, @TRUE, SD_PBKDF2)
   if salted = '' then
      close cred.f
      set.status ER$ARGS
      return
   end

   client.key = SDEXT(salted : @FM : 'Client Key', @TRUE, SD_HMACSHA256)
   stored.key = SDEXT(client.key, @TRUE, SD_SHA256)
   server.key = SDEXT(salted : @FM : 'Server Key', @TRUE, SD_HMACSHA256)

*  Drop the intermediates as soon as they are spent.  Neither may be stored,
*  and ClientKey in particular is the one value that would let its holder
*  authenticate as this account.
   salted = ''
   client.key = ''

   if stored.key = '' or server.key = '' then
      close cred.f
      set.status ER$ARGS
      return
   end

   cred.rec = ''
   cred.rec<CRED$VERSION> = SCRAM$CRED.VERSION
   cred.rec<CRED$MECH> = 'SCRAM-SHA-256'
   cred.rec<CRED$SALT> = salt
   cred.rec<CRED$ITER> = iter
   cred.rec<CRED$STOREDKEY> = stored.key
   cred.rec<CRED$SERVERKEY> = server.key

   write.ok = @true
   write cred.rec to cred.f, account on error
      write.ok = @false
   end
   close cred.f

   if not(write.ok) then
      set.status ER$PERM
      return
   end

*  ***THE READ-BACK IS THE POINT, NOT A COURTESY*** - the port's rule.  A write
*  can report success and leave a record this side cannot use, and an unusable
*  $cred record is an account that authenticates against nothing - found at
*  the next login rather than here.  So the record is read back through a
*  FRESH open and BOTH keys are compared, the same fields the login will use.
*  ER$WRITE.ERROR, not ER$PERM: a write that reported success and does not read
*  back as meant is a different fault from being refused.
   check.rec = ''
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to chk.f else
      set.status ER$NVR
      return
   end
   read check.rec from chk.f, account else check.rec = ''
   close chk.f

   if check.rec<CRED$STOREDKEY> # stored.key then
      set.status ER$WRITE.ERROR
      return
   end

   if check.rec<CRED$SERVERKEY> # server.key then
      set.status ER$WRITE.ERROR
      return
   end

   ok = @true

   return
end

* END-CODE
